{
  "abstract": "A machine-checked proof of Darboux's theorem in the form Kim Morrison stated it for lean-eval (problem darboux): if a 2-form on an open set U of R^2n is smooth, closed and non-degenerate, then every point of U has a neighbourhood with a smooth chart, smooth in both directions, in which the form takes the standard values on the standard basis, so it is the standard symplectic form sum dx_i ^ dx_(n+i). The proof is Moser's: interpolate between the form and its value at the point, write the difference as d(beta) by a Poincare lemma, integrate the vector field X_t with i_(X_t) omega_t = -beta to time 1, and get a chart from the inverse function theorem; a symplectic basis of the form at the point then gives the standard coordinates. Mathlib has no symplectic forms and no Darboux theorem. When lean-eval posed the problem in May 2026 it found no formalization in any proof assistant; since then three other Lean proofs of Darboux's theorem have appeared, each with its own statement (listed under related_formalizations). This proof is independent of them. The theorem is classical and no priority is claimed.",
